## Checklist (in order)

### 0. First run: repo, GitHub and branch

The folder came from a Windows machine, so restore the file modes and line endings that git recorded, then check that the tree is clean:

```bash
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
git config core.filemode true && git config core.symlinks true && git config core.autocrlf false
git read-tree HEAD && git checkout-index -a -f      # rewrites every tracked file with its recorded mode
git status --porcelain                               # must print nothing
test -x whynot-cut/bin/reel && echo "exec bits OK"
```

- `git`: if `git --version` opens the "install command line developer tools" dialog, stop and ask Yunus. That install
  is an Apple system install and may need an admin password. Without admin, `pixi` is the fallback:
  `curl -fsSL https://pixi.sh/install.sh | bash`, then `pixi global install git gh`. It installs everything per user
  into `~/.pixi`.
- `gh`: if it is missing, use `pixi global install gh`, or the official `gh_<version>_macOS_arm64.zip` from
  https://github.com/cli/cli/releases unpacked into `~/.local/`. Then ask Yunus to run `gh auth login` **once**:
  GitHub.com, HTTPS, "Login with a web browser", account **thewhynotai**. Check it with `gh auth status`, then run
  `gh auth setup-git`.
- `git fetch origin`. GitHub may not have every local commit of `{{branch}}` yet; that is expected, so don't "fix" it.
- Create your branch once, then publish it:
  ```bash
  git switch -c mac/dev                    # from {{branch}} @ {{sha_short}}
  git push -u origin mac/dev
  ```
  If `origin/mac/dev` already exists, switch to it instead (`git switch mac/dev` and
  `git pull --ff-only origin mac/dev`). Before every task: `git pull --ff-only`, then re-read `LOCKED-AREAS.md`.

### 1. Local toolchain: no admin, no Homebrew, no system changes

Everything lives in your home folder:
- **uv + Python:** `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh` puts uv in `~/.local/bin`. Then run `uv python install 3.12`, and run tools with `uv run --no-project python ...`.
- **Node** (only to build the `.ccx`): the official `node-v26.*-darwin-arm64.tar.gz` from https://nodejs.org/dist/, checked against that folder's `SHASUMS256.txt`, unpacked into `~/.local/opt/node`, with its `bin/` put on `PATH` in `~/.zprofile`.
- Record every tool's version, and `file $(which <tool>)` (it must say arm64), in the report.

### 2. Build the arm64 Mac artefacts that exist today

- **Plugin zip:** `uv run --no-project python package.py` writes `../whynot-cut-<version>.zip` (next to `repo/`). Run `shasum -a 256` on it.
- **Premiere panel `.ccx`:**
  ```bash
  cd adobe && npm ci && npm run check && npm test && npm run package:plugin
  ```
  This writes `whynot-cut/adobe/WhyNotCut-Premiere-<version>.ccx`. It is gitignored: never commit it. `adobe/**` is locked: build it, but don't edit it.
- **Bridge `.zxp`:** `tools/sign_zxp.py` refuses macOS today, because the macOS pin of Adobe's `ZXPSignCmd` is
  deliberately not set. To add it:
  1. Get the macOS `ZXPSignCmd` from the Adobe-CEP/CEP-Resources repo on GitHub (the same commit as the Windows pin in `PINS`).
  2. Check `file ZXPSignCmd`. If it is x86_64-only, it needs Rosetta: record that as a G-T12 finding and ask Yunus before anything installs Rosetta.
  3. TDD: add a failing test in `whynot-cut/tests/test_sign_zxp.py` for the macOS pin, then add the pin to `PINS` (path, size, sha256).
  4. Run `python tools/sign_zxp.py setup`, then `build`, then `verify`. The self-signed certificate's password goes into the login keychain through `security`, which the tool already supports. Never type a password for Yunus.

  `MAC-INBOX.md` entry: "macOS ZXPSignCmd pin". The Windows side then checks that the Windows pin still works.
- **Not built yet (record as not built, don't invent):** the Setup app, `.app` or `.dmg`. A notarized `.dmg` needs
  the paid Apple Developer Program (H4-Mac); until Yunus decides, the Terminal one-liner is the Mac install path.

### 3. Install from scratch, exactly as a tester would

Use the local zip, through the same piped one-liner path a tester gets. The script has no folder of its own here; that is the `curl ... | bash` mode:
```bash
ZIP="$(cd .. && pwd)/whynot-cut-<version>.zip"; SHA="$(shasum -a 256 "$ZIP" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
bash -s -- --source "$ZIP" --source-sha256 "$SHA" < whynot-cut/installer/install.sh
```
On release day the same thing becomes `curl -fsSL https://dl.thewhynot.ai/install.sh | bash`. That address is not live yet.

